From: baseband lane
Subject: 0506 pandora_mpss: the mailbox window takes 32-bit stores; stage into an aligned buffer

Follow-up to 0505.  The 16-byte vectorised memset was one hazard; the access width is another.

0505 replaced the memset with byte-wise writes through a volatile pointer.  The strict-alignment
fault went away, but the next hardware run left no trace at all -- not a panic (a panic does get
flushed into the pinned ring; the earlier step-4 run proved that), more like a stalled bus and a
watchdog reset.  A register window at offset 0x38 of an AOP mailbox is the kind of block that
expects 32-bit accesses.

So: zero and fill a stack buffer that is explicitly 16-byte aligned (making the memset/memcpy
vectorisation safe), then copy it into the window with 32-bit stores only.

diff --git a/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c b/b/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
index b937dc6..8efbea2 100755
--- a/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
+++ b/bsd/dev/pandora_mpss.c
@@ -719,23 +719,29 @@ mp_mailbox_send(const char *text)
 		len = QMP_MESSAGE - 1;
 	}
 	/*
-	 * Byte-wise through a volatile pointer, deliberately.
+	 * The box is a register window at offset 0x38, so it is 8-byte aligned but NOT 16-byte
+	 * aligned, and like most of this block it wants 32-bit accesses.  Two separate hazards,
+	 * two separate answers:
 	 *
-	 * The mailbox's box is at offset 0x38 of the mapping, so it is 8-byte aligned but NOT
-	 * 16-byte aligned.  `memset(box, 0, 64)` is a 64-byte zeroing that clang vectorises into
-	 * 16-byte NEON stores (`stp q0, q1`), and this kernel runs with strict alignment: the very
-	 * first store faults with "Unaligned kernel data abort", esr 0x96000061, far = box + 0.
-	 * That is exactly what happened on 2026-11-21 at step 3 (pc _step_mailbox + 0x18c), and it
-	 * is why step 4 never reached its TrustZone call.
+	 *   16-byte alignment: `memset(box, 0, 64)` is a 64-byte zeroing that clang vectorises into
+	 *     16-byte NEON stores (`stp q0, q1`), and this kernel runs with strict alignment -- the
+	 *     2026-11-21 fault (esr 0x96000061, far = box + 0) was exactly that.  Fixed by staging
+	 *     into a stack buffer that is explicitly 16-byte aligned.
 	 *
-	 * A byte loop cannot be misaligned whatever the compiler does with it.
+	 *   access width: writing the window byte by byte was the first fix, and on the next run
+	 *     the boot stopped leaving any trace at all (not a panic -- a panic does get flushed to
+	 *     the ring; this looked like a stalled bus and a watchdog reset).  So the window is only
+	 *     ever touched with 32-bit stores, which is what the hardware expects.
 	 */
-	volatile uint8_t *box = (volatile uint8_t *)(mp_qmp + QMP_OUR_BOX);
-	for (unsigned i = 0; i < QMP_MESSAGE; i++) {
-		box[i] = 0;
-	}
-	for (unsigned i = 0; i < len; i++) {
-		box[i] = (uint8_t)text[i];
+	uint8_t staged[QMP_MESSAGE] __attribute__((aligned(16)));
+	memset(staged, 0, sizeof(staged));
+	memcpy(staged, text, len);
+	volatile uint32_t *box = (volatile uint32_t *)(mp_qmp + QMP_OUR_BOX);
+	for (unsigned i = 0; i < QMP_MESSAGE / 4; i++) {
+		uint32_t word;
+
+		memcpy(&word, staged + i * 4, 4);
+		box[i] = word;
 	}
 	__asm__ volatile ("dsb sy" ::: "memory");
 	mp_wr(mp_ipcc, IPCC_SEND, 0u << 16 | 0u);       /* IPCC client 0 (the AOP), signal 0 */
